clk: mediatek: mt6797: model the MCUMIXED CPU clock tree

MT6797 keeps its CPU clocks in a block at 0x1001a000 that mainline has never
described, so the CPU clocks did not exist, mediatek-cpufreq had nothing to
bind to, and the machine ran at whatever operating point LK left behind.
Measured before this: 897 MHz on the little cluster against a rated 1547, and
1274 MHz on the big-little cluster against a rated 2002.

WHAT IS EXPOSED, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY IS NOT

  ARMCAXPLL0  CON1 0x204   little cluster  (cpu0-3, "LL")   exposed
  ARMCAXPLL1  CON1 0x214   big-little      (cpu4-7, "L")    exposed
  ARMCAXPLL2  CON1 0x224   CCI                              NOT exposed
  ARMCAXPLL3  CON1 0x234   spare                            NOT exposed

ARMCAXPLL2 clocks the cache-coherent interconnect. It is live at ~630 MHz and
load-bearing, and nothing here should be able to move it, so it is not
modelled at all. The Cortex-A72 cluster has no entry because it has no PLL the
kernel may drive: the vendor sets that cluster's armpll_addr to NULL and
scales it through the iDVFS co-processor instead (mt_cpufreq.c,
MT_CPU_DVFS_B). Note this corrects the handoff's table, which had ARMCAXPLL2
down as the A72 cluster; the vendor's own per-cluster mapping says CCI.

THE ARITHMETIC, AND THAT IT IS VALIDATED RATHER THAN ASSUMED

    vco  = 26 MHz * pcw / 2^14        pcw    = CON1[20:0]
    rate = vco / (1 << posdiv)        posdiv = CON1[26:24]

Decoded from the running machine it gives 897 MHz and 1274 MHz from
0xC1114000 and 0x400C4000, and 897 MHz is an exact entry in the vendor's OPP
table for this die's speed bin. Driven the other way on #51 the hardware holds
exactly the PCW the arithmetic predicts at every operating point tested --
1547 MHz -> 0x400EE000, 2002 MHz -> 0x40134000, 1209 MHz -> 0xC00BA000.

The postdiv rule is "smallest of 1, 2 or 4 that keeps the VCO at or above
1050 MHz". That is not a guess either: it reproduces the vendor's own POS
column for all 27 operating points of both A53 tables, including the two
places where the vendor switches postdiv mid-table (LL 1118 -> 1014 and
L 1092 -> 962).

CUSTOM PLL OPS RATHER THAN mtk_clk_register_plls()

These PLLs have no power register anywhere in the block, and the vendor never
gates them -- it only ever writes CON1. Rather than invent a power sequence
for the rail the running CPUs clock off, the ops here implement recalc/round/
set_rate only; a clock with no enable op is permanently enabled, which is what
the hardware is. The register write follows the vendor's adjust_armpll_dds()
exactly: one store with PCW_CHG already set, preserving every undecoded bit.

Each PLL also carries the rated maximum for its cluster and refuses anything
above it. The OPP table is the real guard; this is the backstop for the day
the OPP table is wrong, because what is on the far side of that is silicon
running past its voltage.

TWO GATES IN TOPCKGEN, AND WHY THEY MATTER

CLK_MISC_CFG_0 bits 4 and 5 (armpll_divider_pll1_ck_en / pll2) gate the
mainpll and univpll feeds into this block. They are the "intermediate" clock a
cluster parks on while its ARMPLL relocks, and both read back 0 out of the
bootloader -- so reparenting onto mainpll without enabling the gate first
takes the clock away from a running cluster. Modelling them lets the clock
framework enable them for us, in the right order.

Every clock added here is declared so that a rate request cannot walk upwards:
the muxes take CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT and no CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, and the
gates take no flags at all. mainpll clocks the AXI bus, MSDC, I2C and the
UARTs, and issue #56 is what happens when a MediaTek mux is allowed to
reprogram it -- there for the GPU, and the CPU muxes are the same shape.

Verified on #48, which changed nothing: the new clocks read 897.000 MHz and
1274.000 MHz, the muxes report parent index 1 (ARMPLL), and MUXSEL, CKDIV,
CLK_MISC_CFG_0 and both CON1s are bit-for-bit what they were before the
driver existed. Panel gate PASS 13/13.

--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-mcumixed.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797-mcumixed.c
@@ -0,0 +1,375 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * MediaTek MT6797 MCUMIXED CPU clock provider.
+ *
+ * MT6797 keeps the CPU clock tree in its own block at 0x1001a000, entirely
+ * separate from topckgen and apmixedsys, and mainline has never modelled it.
+ * Without it the CPU clocks do not exist and mediatek-cpufreq has nothing to
+ * bind to, which is why this SoC has run at whatever operating point the
+ * bootloader happened to leave behind.
+ *
+ * The block holds four ARMCAXPLLs and one mux-select register:
+ *
+ *   ARMCAXPLL0  CON0 0x200  CON1 0x204   little cluster   (cpu0-3, LL)
+ *   ARMCAXPLL1  CON0 0x210  CON1 0x214   big-little       (cpu4-7, L)
+ *   ARMCAXPLL2  CON0 0x220  CON1 0x224   CCI
+ *   ARMCAXPLL3  CON0 0x230  CON1 0x234   spare
+ *   ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL 0x270   ARMPLLDIV_CKDIV 0x274
+ *
+ * Only ARMCAXPLL0 and ARMCAXPLL1 are exposed. ARMCAXPLL2 clocks the CCI and
+ * is deliberately left alone -- it is live and load-bearing, and nothing here
+ * should be able to touch it. ARMCAXPLL3 is unused. The Cortex-A72 cluster
+ * does NOT have a PLL in this list: the vendor driver sets its armpll_addr to
+ * NULL and drives that cluster's frequency through the iDVFS co-processor
+ * instead (mt_cpufreq.c, MT_CPU_DVFS_B).
+ *
+ * MUXSEL is two bits per cluster -- LL [3:2], L [5:4], B [1:0], CCI [7:6] --
+ * selecting 0 = 26 MHz, 1 = the cluster's own ARMPLL, 2 = mainpll, 3 = univpll.
+ * The mainpll and univpll feeds are separately gated in topckgen's
+ * CLK_MISC_CFG_0, and are exposed there as armpll_div_pll1/armpll_div_pll2;
+ * cpufreq parks the CPU on one of those while its ARMPLL relocks.
+ *
+ * PLL arithmetic, taken from the vendor's _cpu_freq_calc()/_cpu_dds_calc():
+ *
+ *     vco  = 26 MHz * pcw / 2^14        pcw = CON1[20:0]
+ *     rate = vco / (1 << posdiv)        posdiv = CON1[26:24]
+ *
+ * Validated against this silicon in both directions: CON1 = 0xC1114000 decodes
+ * to 897 MHz and 0x400C4000 to 1274 MHz, which is where the bootloader leaves
+ * the two clusters, and 897 MHz is an exact entry in the vendor's OPP table for
+ * this speed bin.
+ *
+ * Copyright (c) 2026 Gemini PDA Linux port
+ */
+
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
+#include <linux/bitops.h>
+#include <linux/clk-provider.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/spinlock.h>
+
+#include <dt-bindings/clock/mt6797-clk.h>
+
+#include "clk-mtk.h"
+
+#define ARMCAXPLL0_CON1		0x204
+#define ARMCAXPLL1_CON1		0x214
+#define ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL	0x270
+#define ARMPLLDIV_CKDIV		0x274
+
+#define ARMPLL_PCW		GENMASK(20, 0)
+#define ARMPLL_POSDIV		GENMASK(26, 24)
+#define ARMPLL_POSDIV_SHIFT	24
+#define ARMPLL_PCW_CHG		BIT(31)
+
+/* pcw is a 7.14 fixed-point multiplier of the 26 MHz reference */
+#define ARMPLL_PCW_FBITS	14
+
+/* vendor PLL_SETTLE_TIME, mt_cpufreq.c */
+#define ARMPLL_SETTLE_US	20
+
+/*
+ * The postdiv the vendor picks for a given target is reproduced exactly by
+ * "smallest postdiv of 1, 2 or 4 that keeps the VCO at or above 1050 MHz".
+ * Checked against every entry of both A53 OPP tables for this speed bin: 27
+ * out of 27 agree, including the two places where the vendor switches postdiv
+ * mid-table (LL 1118 -> 1014, L 1092 -> 962).
+ */
+#define ARMPLL_VCO_MIN		1050000000UL
+
+/* MUXSEL encoding, shared by all four clusters */
+enum {
+	ARMPLL_MUX_CLKSQ	= 0,	/* 26 MHz */
+	ARMPLL_MUX_ARMPLL	= 1,
+	ARMPLL_MUX_MAINPLL	= 2,
+	ARMPLL_MUX_UNIVPLL	= 3,
+};
+
+struct mt6797_armpll {
+	struct clk_hw		hw;
+	void __iomem		*con1;
+	unsigned long		max_rate;
+};
+
+#define to_mt6797_armpll(_hw) container_of(_hw, struct mt6797_armpll, hw)
+
+static unsigned int armpll_postdiv(unsigned long rate)
+{
+	unsigned int pd;
+
+	for (pd = 1; pd < 4; pd <<= 1) {
+		if ((u64)rate * pd >= ARMPLL_VCO_MIN)
+			return pd;
+	}
+
+	return 4;
+}
+
+static u32 armpll_pcw(unsigned long vco, unsigned long fin)
+{
+	return div64_ul((u64)vco << ARMPLL_PCW_FBITS, fin);
+}
+
+static unsigned long armpll_rate(u32 pcw, unsigned int posdiv,
+				 unsigned long fin)
+{
+	return ((u64)fin * pcw >> ARMPLL_PCW_FBITS) >> posdiv;
+}
+
+static unsigned long mt6797_armpll_recalc_rate(struct clk_hw *hw,
+					       unsigned long parent_rate)
+{
+	struct mt6797_armpll *pll = to_mt6797_armpll(hw);
+	u32 con1 = readl(pll->con1);
+
+	return armpll_rate(FIELD_GET(ARMPLL_PCW, con1),
+			   FIELD_GET(ARMPLL_POSDIV, con1), parent_rate);
+}
+
+static long mt6797_armpll_round_rate(struct clk_hw *hw, unsigned long rate,
+				     unsigned long *parent_rate)
+{
+	struct mt6797_armpll *pll = to_mt6797_armpll(hw);
+	unsigned int pd;
+
+	rate = min(rate, pll->max_rate);
+
+	pd = armpll_postdiv(rate);
+
+	return armpll_rate(armpll_pcw(rate * pd, *parent_rate),
+			   ffs(pd) - 1, *parent_rate);
+}
+
+static int mt6797_armpll_set_rate(struct clk_hw *hw, unsigned long rate,
+				  unsigned long parent_rate)
+{
+	struct mt6797_armpll *pll = to_mt6797_armpll(hw);
+	unsigned int pd;
+	u32 con1;
+
+	/*
+	 * A rate above what this cluster is rated for must never reach the
+	 * hardware. The OPP table is the real guard; this is the backstop for
+	 * the day the OPP table is wrong, because the failure mode on the far
+	 * side of it is silicon running past its voltage.
+	 */
+	if (rate > pll->max_rate) {
+		pr_err("%s: refusing %lu Hz, rated maximum is %lu Hz\n",
+		       clk_hw_get_name(hw), rate, pll->max_rate);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	pd = armpll_postdiv(rate);
+
+	/*
+	 * Preserve every bit we have not decoded. Only postdiv and pcw are
+	 * ours; CON1 carries other vendor-defined fields whose reset values
+	 * differ between the two PLLs on this very board.
+	 *
+	 * The write matches the vendor's adjust_armpll_dds(): one store with
+	 * PCW_CHG already set, rather than mainline's store-then-set-CHG pair.
+	 * That is deliberate -- CON1 bit 31 reads back as 1 on ARMCAXPLL0 and 0
+	 * on ARMCAXPLL1 here, so it is plainly not a level the vendor bothers
+	 * to clear, and this is the sequence Android has driven these two PLLs
+	 * with on this silicon.
+	 *
+	 * No postdiv-before/after-pcw ordering dance either: the vendor needs
+	 * it because it reprograms the PLL the CPU is running on, and cpufreq
+	 * has already parked us on mainpll before calling this.
+	 */
+	con1 = readl(pll->con1);
+	con1 &= ~(ARMPLL_POSDIV | ARMPLL_PCW);
+	con1 |= FIELD_PREP(ARMPLL_POSDIV, ffs(pd) - 1);
+	con1 |= FIELD_PREP(ARMPLL_PCW, armpll_pcw(rate * pd, parent_rate));
+	writel(con1 | ARMPLL_PCW_CHG, pll->con1);
+
+	udelay(ARMPLL_SETTLE_US);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * No .enable/.disable/.prepare. These PLLs have no power register anywhere in
+ * this block, the vendor never gates them -- it only ever writes CON1 -- and a
+ * clock with no enable op is treated as permanently enabled. Inventing a
+ * power sequence for the rail the running CPUs clock off is not worth the
+ * symmetry.
+ */
+static const struct clk_ops mt6797_armpll_ops = {
+	.recalc_rate	= mt6797_armpll_recalc_rate,
+	.round_rate	= mt6797_armpll_round_rate,
+	.set_rate	= mt6797_armpll_set_rate,
+};
+
+static const char * const ll_parents[] = {
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_CLKSQ]	= "clk26m",
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_ARMPLL]	= "armcaxpll0",
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_MAINPLL]	= "armpll_div_pll1",
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_UNIVPLL]	= "armpll_div_pll2",
+};
+
+static const char * const l_parents[] = {
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_CLKSQ]	= "clk26m",
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_ARMPLL]	= "armcaxpll1",
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_MAINPLL]	= "armpll_div_pll1",
+	[ARMPLL_MUX_UNIVPLL]	= "armpll_div_pll2",
+};
+
+struct mt6797_armpll_desc {
+	unsigned int	id;
+	const char	*name;
+	u32		con1;
+	unsigned long	max_rate;
+};
+
+/*
+ * Rated maxima are the top entry of each cluster's OPP table for the speed
+ * bin this die reports (SB): LL 1547 MHz, L 2002 MHz.
+ */
+static const struct mt6797_armpll_desc mt6797_armplls[] = {
+	{ CLK_MCU_ARMCAXPLL0, "armcaxpll0", ARMCAXPLL0_CON1, 1547000000UL },
+	{ CLK_MCU_ARMCAXPLL1, "armcaxpll1", ARMCAXPLL1_CON1, 2002000000UL },
+};
+
+struct mt6797_cpumux_desc {
+	unsigned int		id;
+	const char		*name;
+	const char * const	*parents;
+	u8			shift;
+};
+
+static const struct mt6797_cpumux_desc mt6797_cpumuxes[] = {
+	{ CLK_MCU_LL_SEL, "mcu_ll_sel", ll_parents, 2 },
+	{ CLK_MCU_L_SEL,  "mcu_l_sel",  l_parents,  4 },
+};
+
+/* LL is CKDIV[9:5], L is CKDIV[14:10] */
+#define CKDIV_LL		GENMASK(9, 5)
+#define CKDIV_L			GENMASK(14, 10)
+
+static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(mt6797_mcumixed_lock);
+
+static int mt6797_mcumixed_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct clk_hw_onecell_data *clk_data;
+	void __iomem *base;
+	u32 ckdiv;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	base = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
+	if (IS_ERR(base))
+		return PTR_ERR(base);
+
+	/*
+	 * ARMPLLDIV_CKDIV is a fractional post-divider the vendor uses only for
+	 * the handful of OPPs below what postdiv alone can reach. We model none
+	 * of it and the bootloader leaves it at zero. If it is ever not zero
+	 * then every rate this driver reports is wrong by an unknown fraction,
+	 * and wrong rates paired with real voltages is how CPUs die -- so
+	 * refuse to register rather than hand cpufreq a lie.
+	 */
+	ckdiv = readl(base + ARMPLLDIV_CKDIV);
+	if (ckdiv & (CKDIV_LL | CKDIV_L)) {
+		dev_err(dev, "ARMPLLDIV_CKDIV = %#x: an A53 cluster is running through the fractional divider, which this driver does not model\n",
+			ckdiv);
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	clk_data = mtk_alloc_clk_data(CLK_MCU_NR);
+	if (!clk_data)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(mt6797_armplls); i++) {
+		const struct mt6797_armpll_desc *d = &mt6797_armplls[i];
+		struct clk_init_data init = {};
+		struct mt6797_armpll *pll;
+
+		pll = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*pll), GFP_KERNEL);
+		if (!pll) {
+			ret = -ENOMEM;
+			goto err;
+		}
+
+		pll->con1 = base + d->con1;
+		pll->max_rate = d->max_rate;
+
+		init.name = d->name;
+		init.ops = &mt6797_armpll_ops;
+		init.parent_names = (const char *[]){ "clk26m" };
+		init.num_parents = 1;
+		pll->hw.init = &init;
+
+		ret = devm_clk_hw_register(dev, &pll->hw);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(dev, "failed to register %s: %d\n",
+				d->name, ret);
+			goto err;
+		}
+
+		clk_data->hws[d->id] = &pll->hw;
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(mt6797_cpumuxes); i++) {
+		const struct mt6797_cpumux_desc *d = &mt6797_cpumuxes[i];
+		struct clk_hw *hw;
+
+		/*
+		 * CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, and no CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT.
+		 *
+		 * cpufreq reparents this mux itself, deliberately, and sets the
+		 * rate on the ARMPLL it fetches with clk_get_parent(). What it
+		 * must never be able to do is serve a rate request by moving
+		 * onto mainpll or univpll and reprogramming one of them -- both
+		 * clock the whole die. That is not hypothetical: it is issue
+		 * #56 exactly, where CLK_TOP_MUX_MFG was allowed to do it for
+		 * the GPU and took every other bus master down with it.
+		 */
+		hw = devm_clk_hw_register_mux(dev, d->name, d->parents, 4,
+					      CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT,
+					      base + ARMPLLDIV_MUXSEL,
+					      d->shift, 2, 0,
+					      &mt6797_mcumixed_lock);
+		if (IS_ERR(hw)) {
+			dev_err(dev, "failed to register %s: %pe\n",
+				d->name, hw);
+			ret = PTR_ERR(hw);
+			goto err;
+		}
+
+		clk_data->hws[d->id] = hw;
+	}
+
+	ret = of_clk_add_hw_provider(dev->of_node, of_clk_hw_onecell_get,
+				     clk_data);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err;
+
+	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, clk_data);
+
+	return 0;
+
+err:
+	mtk_free_clk_data(clk_data);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id of_match_clk_mt6797_mcumixed[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-mcumixed" },
+	{ /* sentinel */ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, of_match_clk_mt6797_mcumixed);
+
+static struct platform_driver clk_mt6797_mcumixed_drv = {
+	.probe = mt6797_mcumixed_probe,
+	.driver = {
+		.name = "clk-mt6797-mcumixed",
+		.of_match_table = of_match_clk_mt6797_mcumixed,
+	},
+};
+builtin_platform_driver(clk_mt6797_mcumixed_drv);
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/Kconfig
@@ -343,6 +343,18 @@
 	help
 	  This driver supports MediaTek MT6797 vencsys clocks.
 
+config COMMON_CLK_MT6797_MCUMIXED
+	bool "Clock driver for MediaTek MT6797 mcumixed (CPU clocks)"
+	depends on COMMON_CLK_MT6797
+	default COMMON_CLK_MT6797
+	help
+	  This driver supports the MediaTek MT6797 MCUMIXED block, which holds
+	  the ARMCAXPLLs and the per-cluster CPU clock muxes. Without it the
+	  CPU clocks do not exist and mediatek-cpufreq cannot bind, so the CPUs
+	  run at whatever operating point the bootloader left behind.
+
+	  Built in rather than modular: cpufreq needs these clocks at boot.
+
 config COMMON_CLK_MT7622
 	tristate "Clock driver for MediaTek MT7622"
 	depends on ARCH_MEDIATEK || COMPILE_TEST
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/Makefile
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6795_VDECSYS) += clk-mt6795-vdecsys.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6795_VENCSYS) += clk-mt6795-vencsys.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6797) += clk-mt6797.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6797_MCUMIXED) += clk-mt6797-mcumixed.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6797_IMGSYS) += clk-mt6797-img.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6797_MFGCFG) += clk-mt6797-mfg.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6797_MMSYS) += clk-mt6797-mm.o
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/mt6797-clk.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/mt6797-clk.h
@@ -102,7 +102,21 @@
 #define	CLK_TOP_MSDCPLL_D2			92
 #define	CLK_TOP_MSDCPLL_D4			93
 #define	CLK_TOP_MSDCPLL_D8			94
-#define	CLK_TOP_NR				95
+/*
+ * The mainpll and univpll feeds into the MCUMIXED ARMPLL divider block,
+ * CLK_MISC_CFG_0 bits 4 and 5. cpufreq parks a CPU cluster on one of these
+ * while its own ARMPLL relocks.
+ */
+#define	CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1			95
+#define	CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL2			96
+#define	CLK_TOP_NR				97
+
+/* MCUMIXED (mcumixed@1001a000) -- the CPU clock tree */
+#define CLK_MCU_ARMCAXPLL0			0
+#define CLK_MCU_ARMCAXPLL1			1
+#define CLK_MCU_LL_SEL				2
+#define CLK_MCU_L_SEL				3
+#define CLK_MCU_NR				4
 
 /* APMIXED_SYS */
 #define CLK_APMIXED_MAINPLL			1
--- a/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/mediatek/clk-mt6797.c
@@ -420,6 +420,27 @@
 				    ARRAY_SIZE(top_muxes), base,
 				    &mt6797_clk_lock, clk_data);
 
+	/*
+	 * CLK_MISC_CFG_0 bit 4 (armpll_divider_pll1_ck_en) and bit 5
+	 * (armpll_divider_pll2_ck_en) gate the mainpll and univpll feeds into
+	 * the MCUMIXED ARMPLL divider block. They are the "intermediate" clock
+	 * a CPU cluster parks on while its own ARMPLL relocks, and both read
+	 * back 0 out of the bootloader -- so a clk_set_parent onto mainpll
+	 * without enabling the gate first takes the clock away from a running
+	 * cluster. Modelling them here lets the clock framework enable them for
+	 * us, in the right order, when cpufreq prepares the intermediate clock.
+	 *
+	 * Plain gates, flags 0: no CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT. A rate request must
+	 * never be allowed to walk up from a CPU cluster into mainpll, which
+	 * clocks the AXI bus, MSDC, I2C and the UARTs (issue #56).
+	 */
+	clk_data->hws[CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL1] =
+		clk_hw_register_gate(&pdev->dev, "armpll_div_pll1", "syspll_ck",
+				     0, base + 0x104, 4, 0, &mt6797_clk_lock);
+	clk_data->hws[CLK_TOP_ARMPLL_DIV_PLL2] =
+		clk_hw_register_gate(&pdev->dev, "armpll_div_pll2", "univpll_ck",
+				     0, base + 0x104, 5, 0, &mt6797_clk_lock);
+
 	return of_clk_add_hw_provider(node, of_clk_hw_onecell_get, clk_data);
 }
 
